IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `wra-core`

-- Welfare Reform Act 2012 (United Kingdom), 2012 c. 5, Part 5, ss 96, 96A and 97: the benefit cap.
-- Layer 1 (the law, objectively), part wra4. Source: inputs/ukpga-2012-5.txt, a plain-text rendering made by a
-- converter script; it is not the official text. A line `-- src:N | text` copies raw line N.
-- Host Acts named in labels: SSA1998 Social Security Act 1998; SSAA Social Security Administration Act 1992.
--
-- Reading a result (the rule of 2026-10-29, assumed, not ruled by a lawyer):
--   JUST FALSE / FALSE  = the text says no in its own words (a "means" list, a flat negative, an "unless"/"only if").
--   NOTHING             = not covered: the text is silent, points to regulations, or limits its own grant.
--   A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- Seams taken as inputs (PLAN.md section 8): seam 2 (the amount of each universal credit award, from s 8(1)) is the
-- `the amount awarded for the period, in pounds` field of an Award of universal credit.

§ `s 96 -- the benefit cap`

-- "welfare benefit" is defined by a closed list under "means": a benefit the list leaves out is not one.
-- src:1556 | “welfare benefit” means—
-- src:1557 | bereavement allowance (see section 39B of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992),
-- src:1558 | child benefit (see section 141 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992),
-- src:1559 | child tax credit (see section 1(1)(a) of the Tax Credits Act 2002),
-- src:1560 | employment and support allowance (see section 1 of the Welfare Reform Act 2007), including income-related employment and support allowance (as defined in section 1(7) of the Welfare Reform Act 2007),
-- src:1561 | housing benefit (see section 130 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992),
-- src:1562 | incapacity benefit (see section 30A of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992),
-- src:1563 | income support (see section 124 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992),
-- src:1564 | jobseeker's allowance (see section 1 of the Jobseekers Act 1995), including income-based jobseeker's allowance (as defined in section 1(4) of the Jobseekers Act 1995),
-- src:1565 | maternity allowance under section 35 or 35B of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992,
-- src:1566 | severe disablement allowance (see section 68 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992),
-- src:1567 | universal credit,
-- src:1568 | widow's pension (see section 38 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992),
-- src:1569 | widowed mother's allowance (see section 37 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992), or
-- src:1570 | widowed parent's allowance (see section 39A of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992).
@ref s 96(10) “welfare benefit” (src:1556-1570)
GIVEN `the benefit` IS A Benefit
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 96(10) the benefit is a welfare benefit` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the benefit`
    WHEN `universal credit` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `contribution-based jobseeker's allowance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `income-based jobseeker's allowance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `contributory employment and support allowance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `income-related employment and support allowance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `income support` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `housing benefit` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `child tax credit` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `child benefit` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `bereavement allowance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `incapacity benefit` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `maternity allowance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `severe disablement allowance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `widow's pension` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `widowed mother's allowance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `widowed parent's allowance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `council tax benefit` THEN FALSE                 -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“welfare benefit” means—" (closed list; not on it)
    WHEN `working tax credit` THEN FALSE                  -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“welfare benefit” means—" (only child tax credit is on it)
    WHEN `state pension credit` THEN FALSE                -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“welfare benefit” means—" (closed list; not on it)
    WHEN `housing credit` THEN FALSE                      -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“welfare benefit” means—" (closed list; not on it)
    WHEN `personal independence payment` THEN FALSE       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“welfare benefit” means—" (closed list; not on it)
    WHEN `disability living allowance` THEN FALSE         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“welfare benefit” means—" (closed list; not on it)
    WHEN `industrial injuries benefit` THEN FALSE         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“welfare benefit” means—" (closed list; not on it)
    WHEN `social fund payment` THEN FALSE                 -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“welfare benefit” means—" (closed list; not on it)
    WHEN `guardian's allowance` THEN FALSE                -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“welfare benefit” means—" (closed list; not on it)

-- "couple" means two persons "of a prescribed description": the description is left to regulations, so it is an input.
-- This is s 96's own definition; it is not connected to s 39 (PLAN.md section 8, "not seams").
-- src:1552 | “couple” means two persons of a prescribed description;
@ref s 96(10) “couple” (src:1552-1552)
GIVEN `the two persons are of a description regulations prescribe` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Are the two persons of a description that the benefit cap regulations prescribe as a couple? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 96(10) the two persons are a couple` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the two persons are of a description regulations prescribe`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE                       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“couple” means two persons of a prescribed description"
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the description is prescribed in regulations

-- src:1555 | “single person” means a person who is not a member of a couple;
@ref s 96(10) “single person” (src:1555-1555)
GIVEN `the person is a member of a couple in the sense of s 96(10)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Is the person a member of a couple as the benefit cap regulations describe a couple? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 96(10) the person is a single person` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the person is a member of a couple in the sense of s 96(10)`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE                        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“single person” means a person who is not a member of a couple"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether the person is in a couple turns on the prescribed description

-- "regulations" means regulations made by the Secretary of State: a closed definition.
-- src:1554 | “regulations” means regulations made by the Secretary of State;
@ref s 96(10) “regulations” (src:1554-1554)
GIVEN `the maker` IS AN Authority
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 96(10) regulations made by this maker are regulations` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the maker`
    WHEN `the Secretary of State` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an authorised person` THEN FALSE                -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“regulations” means regulations made by the Secretary of State"
    WHEN `an employment officer` THEN FALSE              -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“regulations” means regulations made by the Secretary of State"
    WHEN `Her Majesty's Revenue and Customs` THEN FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“regulations” means regulations made by the Secretary of State"
    WHEN `a local authority` THEN FALSE                   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“regulations” means regulations made by the Secretary of State"
    WHEN `the First-tier Tribunal` THEN FALSE             -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“regulations” means regulations made by the Secretary of State"
    WHEN `the Upper Tribunal` THEN FALSE                  -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“regulations” means regulations made by the Secretary of State"
    WHEN `a court` THEN FALSE                             -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“regulations” means regulations made by the Secretary of State"
    WHEN `the Director of Public Prosecutions` THEN FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“regulations” means regulations made by the Secretary of State"

-- "prescribed" means prescribed in regulations (and "regulations" are those the Secretary of State makes).
-- src:1553 | “prescribed” means prescribed in regulations;
@ref s 96(10) “prescribed” (src:1553-1553)
GIVEN `the matter is set out in an instrument` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the matter set out in an instrument made under s 96?
      `the maker of the instrument` IS AN Authority  @desc Who made the instrument?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 96(10) the matter is prescribed` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "“prescribed” means prescribed in regulations" (not set out, or not made by the Secretary of State)
    `the matter is set out in an instrument` AND `s 96(10) regulations made by this maker are regulations` `the maker of the instrument`

-- s 96(5A): the annual limit is one of two pairs of figures; s 96(5B)(a) leaves it to regulations to say which figure of
-- the pair applies to which description of person, and s 96(5B)(b) leaves "resident" to regulations.
DECLARE `s 96(5A) figure of a pair` IS ONE OF
    `s 96(5A) the first figure`
    `s 96(5A) the second figure`

-- src:1535 | (5A) For the purposes of this section the “annual limit” is—
-- src:1536 | (a) £25,323 or £16,967, for persons resident in Greater London;
-- src:1537 | (b) £22,020 or £14,753, for other persons.
@ref s 96(5A) (src:1535-1537)
GIVEN `the person is resident in Greater London as regulations define resident` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Do you live in Greater London, in the sense of "resident" that the benefit cap regulations define? Leave out if not known.
      `the figure regulations specify for the person` IS A MAYBE `s 96(5A) figure of a pair`  @desc Which figure of the pair do the regulations specify for the person (the first or the second)? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 96(5A) the annual limit` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the person is resident in Greater London as regulations define resident`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "resident" is defined by regulations (s 96(5B)(b))
    WHEN JUST london THEN
        CONSIDER `the figure regulations specify for the person`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                         -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): which figure of the pair applies is left to regulations (s 96(5B)(a))
        WHEN JUST figure THEN
            IF london
            THEN (CONSIDER figure
                  WHEN `s 96(5A) the first figure` THEN JUST 25323
                  WHEN `s 96(5A) the second figure` THEN JUST 16967)
            ELSE (CONSIDER figure
                  WHEN `s 96(5A) the first figure` THEN JUST 22020
                  WHEN `s 96(5A) the second figure` THEN JUST 14753)

-- s 96(5B)(c): regulations may round the annual limit divided by the number of reference periods in a year, up or down.
-- The text names no unit of rounding, so the unit is part of the regulation's choice.
DECLARE `s 96(5B)(c) rounding` IS ONE OF
    `s 96(5B)(c) rounded up to a multiple of`   HAS `the unit in pounds` IS A NUMBER
    `s 96(5B)(c) rounded down to a multiple of` HAS `the unit in pounds` IS A NUMBER

-- src:1543 | (c) provide for the rounding up or down of an amount produced by dividing the amount of the annual limit by the number of periods of a duration equal to the reference period in a year.
@ref s 96(5B)(c) (src:1543-1543)
GIVEN `the annual limit` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of periods of the reference period's duration in a year` IS A NUMBER
      `the rounding regulations provide` IS A MAYBE `s 96(5B)(c) rounding`
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 96(5B)(c) the annual limit divided into reference periods` MEANS
    IF `the number of periods of the reference period's duration in a year` AT MOST 0
    THEN NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): there is no such division by no periods
    ELSE CONSIDER `the rounding regulations provide`
         WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether and how to round is left to regulations
         WHEN JUST r THEN
             CONSIDER r
             WHEN `s 96(5B)(c) rounded up to a multiple of` unit
                 THEN JUST ((CEILING ((`the annual limit` DIVIDED BY `the number of periods of the reference period's duration in a year`) DIVIDED BY unit)) TIMES unit)
             WHEN `s 96(5B)(c) rounded down to a multiple of` unit
                 THEN JUST ((FLOOR ((`the annual limit` DIVIDED BY `the number of periods of the reference period's duration in a year`) DIVIDED BY unit)) TIMES unit)

-- s 96(3): "reference period" means a period of a prescribed duration. Taken here as whole days (the unit is not stated).
-- src:1525 | (3) In subsection (2) the “reference period” means a period of a prescribed duration.
@ref s 96(3) (src:1525-1525)
GIVEN `the first day of the reference period` IS A DATE
      `the duration regulations prescribe, in days` IS A MAYBE NUMBER  @desc For how many days do the benefit cap regulations measure the reference period? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE Period
`s 96(3) the reference period` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the duration regulations prescribe, in days`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "a period of a prescribed duration"
    WHEN JUST days THEN
        IF days AT LEAST 1
        THEN JUST (Period WITH `first day` IS `the first day of the reference period`, `last day` IS (`the first day of the reference period` PLUS (days MINUS 1)))
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): a duration of less than one day is no period

-- s 96(2): total entitlement to welfare benefits in respect of the reference period, from the awards (each an Award; for
-- universal credit the amount is the s 8(1) amount, seam 2). Only welfare benefits count (s 96(10)).
-- src:1524 | (2) For the purposes of this section, applying a benefit cap to welfare benefits means securing that, where a single person's or couple's total entitlement to welfare benefits in respect of the reference period exceeds the relevant amount, their entitlement to welfare benefits in respect of any period of the same duration as the reference period is reduced by an amount up to or equalling the excess.
@ref s 96(2) (src:1524-1524)
GIVEN `the awards in respect of the reference period` IS A LIST OF Award
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 96(2) the total entitlement to welfare benefits` MEANS
    sum (map (GIVEN a YIELD a's `the amount awarded for the period, in pounds`)
             (filter (GIVEN a YIELD `s 96(10) the benefit is a welfare benefit` (a's `the benefit awarded`)) `the awards in respect of the reference period`))

-- "exceeds" is strict: a total equal to the relevant amount is not an excess.
@ref s 96(2) (src:1524-1524)
GIVEN `the total entitlement` IS A NUMBER
      `the relevant amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 96(2) the total entitlement exceeds the relevant amount` MEANS
    `the total entitlement` GREATER THAN `the relevant amount`

-- The cap "reduces" entitlement "by an amount up to or equalling the excess": the excess is the greatest reduction.
@ref s 96(2) (src:1524-1524)
GIVEN `the total entitlement` IS A NUMBER
      `the relevant amount` IS A MAYBE NUMBER  @desc What is the relevant amount for the reference period under the benefit cap regulations? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 96(2) the greatest reduction the cap can require` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the relevant amount`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the relevant amount is determined by regulations (s 96(5))
    WHEN JUST amount THEN
        IF `s 96(2) the total entitlement exceeds the relevant amount` `the total entitlement` amount
        THEN JUST (`the total entitlement` MINUS amount)
        ELSE NOTHING                                      -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the cap reduces entitlement only "where" the total exceeds the relevant amount

-- s 96(1) with s 96(4)(c): regulations may provide for a cap, and may provide exceptions to its application.
-- src:1523 | (1) Regulations may provide for a benefit cap to be applied to the welfare benefits to which a single person or couple is entitled.
-- src:1529 | (c) provide for exceptions to the application of the benefit cap;
@ref s 96(1) and s 96(4)(c) (src:1523-1529)
GIVEN `regulations provide for a benefit cap` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Do regulations provide for a benefit cap to be applied to the benefits of a person in your position? Leave out if not known.
      `regulations except the person from the cap` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN  @desc Do the regulations make an exception that covers you? Leave out if not known.
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 96(1) a benefit cap is applied to the person's welfare benefits` MEANS
    CONSIDER `regulations provide for a benefit cap`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                             -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Regulations may provide for a benefit cap"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING                          -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text does not say that no cap applies where regulations do not provide one
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        CONSIDER `regulations except the person from the cap`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING                         -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): exceptions are for regulations (s 96(4)(c))
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE                    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "provide for exceptions to the application of the benefit cap"
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE

-- s 96(9): regulations may not reduce a welfare benefit that is devolved.
-- src:1547 | (9) Regulations under this section may not provide for any reduction to be made from a welfare benefit—
-- src:1548 | (a) provision for which is within the legislative competence of the Scottish Parliament;
-- src:1549 | (b) provision for which is within the legislative competence of the National Assembly for Wales;
-- src:1550 | (c) provision for which is made by the Welsh Ministers, the First Minister for Wales or the Counsel General to the Welsh Assembly Government.
@ref s 96(9) (src:1547-1550)
GIVEN `the benefit's provision is within the legislative competence of the Scottish Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the provision for this benefit within the legislative competence of the Scottish Parliament?
      `the benefit's provision is within the legislative competence of the National Assembly for Wales` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the provision for this benefit within the legislative competence of the National Assembly for Wales?
      `the benefit's provision is made by the Welsh Ministers, the First Minister for Wales or the Counsel General` IS A BOOLEAN  @desc Is the provision for this benefit made by the Welsh Ministers, the First Minister for Wales or the Counsel General to the Welsh Assembly Government?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 96(9) benefit cap regulations may reduce this welfare benefit` MEANS
    IF `the benefit's provision is within the legislative competence of the Scottish Parliament`
       OR `the benefit's provision is within the legislative competence of the National Assembly for Wales`
       OR `the benefit's provision is made by the Welsh Ministers, the First Minister for Wales or the Counsel General`
    THEN JUST FALSE                                       -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Regulations under this section may not provide for any reduction to be made from a welfare benefit—"
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): the bar covers only the three kinds of benefit named; otherwise the text does not say a reduction may be made

§ `s 96A -- review of the annual limit`

-- s 96A(1): the Secretary of State "must at least once every five years" review the sums. Read as: no more than five years
-- from the last review (the fifth anniversary included); see the fork register. `add years` clamps 29 February to 28.
-- src:1573 | (1) The Secretary of State must at least once every five years review the sums specified in section 96(5A) to determine whether it is appropriate to increase or decrease any one or more of those sums.
@ref s 96A(1) (src:1573-1573)
GIVEN `the date of the last review` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 96A(1) the latest day for the next review of the sums` MEANS
    `add years` `the date of the last review` 5

@ref s 96A(1) (src:1573-1573)
GIVEN `the date of the last review` IS A DATE
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 96A(1) the five-year review is overdue` MEANS
    `the day in question` GREATER THAN `s 96A(1) the latest day for the next review of the sums` `the date of the last review`

-- s 96A(2): at any other time the Secretary of State "considers appropriate" (his judgment is a plain input).
-- src:1574 | (2) The Secretary of State may, at any other time the Secretary of State considers appropriate, review the sums specified in section 96(5A) to determine whether it is appropriate to increase or decrease any one or more of those sums.
@ref s 96A(2) (src:1574-1574)
GIVEN `the Secretary of State considers it appropriate to review now` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 96A(2) the Secretary of State may review the sums at this time` MEANS
    IF `the Secretary of State considers it appropriate to review now`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING                                          -- NOTHING-kind (limited by own wording): "at any other time the Secretary of State considers appropriate"

-- s 96A(3): in a review the Secretary of State "must take into account" the economic situation and what he considers relevant.
-- src:1575 | (3) In carrying out a review, the Secretary of State must take into account—
-- src:1576 | (a) the national economic situation, and
-- src:1577 | (b) any other matters that the Secretary of State considers relevant.
@ref s 96A(3) (src:1575-1577)
GIVEN `the review took into account the national economic situation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 96A(3) the review met the requirement to take account of the national economic situation` MEANS
    `the review took into account the national economic situation`

§ `s 97 -- supplementary: no appeal against the cap decision`

-- s 97(6): the host Act's Schedule 2 is headed "decisions against which no appeal lies"; para 8A adds the decision to apply
-- the benefit cap. The rule is stated for the decision kinds in the core; any other decision is outside this provision.
-- src:1596 | (6) In Schedule 2 to the Social Security Act 1998 (decisions against which no appeal lies) after paragraph 8 there is inserted—
-- src:1597 | 8A Reduction on application of benefit cap
-- src:1598 | A decision to apply the benefit cap in accordance with regulations under section 96 of the Welfare Reform Act 2012.
@ref s 97(6) SSA1998 Sch 2 para 8A (src:1596-1598)
GIVEN `the decision` IS A Decision
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 97(6) SSA1998 Sch 2 para 8A an appeal lies against the decision` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the decision`'s `the kind of decision`
    WHEN `a decision to apply the benefit cap` THEN JUST FALSE  -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "decisions against which no appeal lies" (Schedule 2 para 8A: "A decision to apply the benefit cap")
    WHEN `a decision on entitlement to a benefit or on its amount` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent): para 8A names only the benefit cap decision
    WHEN `a decision on limited capability for work or work-related activity` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent): para 8A names only the benefit cap decision
    WHEN `a decision on ability to carry out daily living or mobility activities` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent): para 8A names only the benefit cap decision
    WHEN `a decision that an amount is recoverable or on how much` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent): para 8A names only the benefit cap decision
    WHEN `a decision on the amount payable after loss of benefit for an offence` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (silent): para 8A names only the benefit cap decision
